findings.body:
Before this PR, the current-user exception only applied when the filter was **exactly** the caller's own ID:

`filters.userIds.length === 1 && user.id === filters.userIds[0]`

The PR changes it to `filters.userIds.includes(user.id)`:

::diff{file="packages/trpc/server/routers/viewer/bookings/get.handler.ts" lines="144-159"}

The guard throws only when `!areUserIdsWithinUserOrgOrTeam && !isCurrentUser`. Any request whose `userIds` includes the caller's own ID therefore skips the permission check completely. The queries that follow then use the unchecked list:

- Query 1: `.where("userId", "in", filters.userIds)` returns every booking hosted by every listed user.
- Queries 2–3: attendee/seat matches on the emails of every listed user, from `getAttendeeEmailsFromUserIdsFilter`.

::callout{type="security"}
Exploit: any logged-in user with no team role sends `filters: { userIds: [<myId>, <victimId>] }` and gets the victim's bookings, including attendee details. This works across organizations.

Until this PR, a request mixing your own ID with someone else's still had to pass the team check. Fix it by restoring the exact-match semantics, or by authorizing each ID separately:

::suggestion{file="packages/trpc/server/routers/viewer/bookings/get.handler.ts" lines="145-149"}
    const areUserIdsWithinUserOrgOrTeam = filters.userIds.every(
      (userId) => userId === user.id || allAccessibleUserIds.includes(userId)
    );

    const isCurrentUser = filters.userIds.length === 1 && user.id === filters.userIds[0];

None of the new tests send a mixed list like `[1, 4]`, so this regression isn't caught. Add a test that expects `FORBIDDEN` for that case.

The singular repository method gained an `orgId` parameter, but it still calls the plural method without it:

::diff{file="packages/features/pbac/infrastructure/repositories/PermissionRepository.ts" lines="211-223"}

`getBookings` calls `permissionCheckService.getTeamIdsWithPermission({ ..., orgId: user.orgId ?? undefined })`. The service passes `orgId` on, but the repository discards it at this point. The SQL always runs with `orgId = NULL`, which means "all teams".

The old handler filtered memberships to `teamId = user.orgId OR team.parentId = user.orgId`. This is a regression: an org admin who is also ADMIN/OWNER of a team outside the current org (another org, or a standalone team) now sees that team's members' bookings in the current org context.

The new integration tests only call `getTeamIdsWithPermissions` (plural), and the unit tests mock the service, so this gap isn't caught.

::suggestion{file="packages/features/pbac/infrastructure/repositories/PermissionRepository.ts" lines="222-222"}
    return this.getTeamIdsWithPermissions({ userId, permissions: [permission], fallbackRoles, orgId });

In the child-team branches, `child."parentId" = org.id` always holds because of the join. That means `child."parentId" = ${orgId}` repeats `org."id" = ${orgId}`. Meanwhile `child."id" = ${orgId}` only matches when the scoped org is itself a child of a team the user belongs to, which an organization never is:

::code{file="packages/features/pbac/infrastructure/repositories/PermissionRepository.ts" lines="355-358"}

The two branches also use different predicates: PBAC at line 309 and fallback at line 357. A reader has to stop and work out whether that's intentional. `AND (${orgId}::bigint IS NULL OR org."id" = ${orgId})` expresses the intent in both. The fallback JSDoc also has the `@param orgId` line twice.
